WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.154
Bypassing iOS Safari's

00:00:01.977 --> 00:00:03.475
60 FPS limit

00:00:03.345 --> 00:00:04.324
by driving web

00:00:04.135 --> 00:00:05.078
animations

00:00:05.598 --> 00:00:06.730
Rida F'kih shows

00:00:06.541 --> 00:00:07.154
how to get

00:00:06.966 --> 00:00:09.300
120 Hz-feeling interactions

00:00:09.112 --> 00:00:10.409
in iOS Safari

00:00:10.622 --> 00:00:12.343
where requestAnimationFrame

00:00:12.308 --> 00:00:13.333
and touch events

00:00:13.228 --> 00:00:14.088
are capped at

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:14.961
60 Hz

00:00:15.478 --> 00:00:17.140
Scrolling however runs

00:00:16.952 --> 00:00:17.895
on the compositor

00:00:17.707 --> 00:00:18.272
thread

00:00:18.462 --> 00:00:19.204
so the post

00:00:19.016 --> 00:00:20.171
treats a scrollable

00:00:19.983 --> 00:00:20.784
region as a

00:00:20.596 --> 00:00:21.468
trackpad

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:22.376
trackball

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:23.921
bouncing surface or

00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:25.018
notched dial using

00:00:24.829 --> 00:00:25.643
momentum

00:00:25.749 --> 00:00:27.317
overscroll and snap

00:00:27.129 --> 00:00:27.883
points

00:00:28.406 --> 00:00:29.644
Because scroll events

00:00:29.585 --> 00:00:30.717
only arrived 5

00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:31.719
to 26 times

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:32.497
per second during

00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:33.488
momentum scrolls

00:00:34.014 --> 00:00:35.028
Full story on

00:00:34.839 --> 00:00:35.735
the dashboard
